--TEST--
A `match` over an enum or a nullable enum with no `default` arm names every case, and `null` for a nullable enum (`rule:expressions/a-match-over-an-enum-covers-every-case`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/match-and-switch-over-an-enum
// A statically typed enum is always one of its cases, so a `match` with no
// `default` that misses a case is the same mistake as a missing `return`. The
// error is at the `match` keyword and lists every missing case. A `?Priority`
// subject also needs an arm for `null`.
enum Priority { Low, Medium, High }

final class Labels {
    // Refused: `Medium` and `High` have no arm.
    public static function short(Priority $p): string {
        return match ($p) {
            Priority::Low => "low",
        };
    }

    // Refused: every case has an arm, but `null` has none.
    public static function maybe(?Priority $p): string {
        return match ($p) {
            Priority::Low, Priority::Medium => "not high",
            Priority::High => "high",
        };
    }

    // Accepted: a `default` arm handles every other case.
    public static function loose(Priority $p): string {
        return match ($p) {
            Priority::Low => "low",
            default => "other",
        };
    }
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E1016]: this `match` does not handle `Priority::Medium` and `Priority::High`
   --> case.nvs:12:16
%A
error[E1016]: this `match` does not handle `null`
   --> case.nvs:19:16
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
